<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · monument</p><h1 class="enc-title">Ta Prohm</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1186">1186</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/12th-century">12th century</a> · Also known as Rajavihara</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Ta Prohm is a temple at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/angkor">Angkor</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cambodia">Cambodia</a>, founded by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jayavarman-vii">Jayavarman VII</a> in 1186 as a Buddhist monastery dedicated to his mother. Its foundation inscription lists thousands of villages and tens of thousands of people who supplied and served it, a measure of the economic weight of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khmer-empire">Khmer</a> religious foundations. Left largely as it was found, with giant trees growing out of its galleries, it is one of the best-known sites of Angkor.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/ta-prohm.jpg" alt="Ta Prohm" width="320" height="213" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: This Photo was taken by Supanut Arunoprayote. Feel free to use any of my images, but please mention me as the author ... · CC BY 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Ta_Prohm_(III).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>Ta Prohm is the modern name, meaning &quot;Ancestor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brahma">Brahma</a>&quot;, of a temple about one kilometre east of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/angkor-thom">Angkor Thom</a> and on the southern edge of the East Baray, near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/siem-reap">Siem Reap</a>. It was built in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bayon">Bayon</a> style, largely in the late twelfth and early thirteenth centuries, and was originally called Rajavihara, the &quot;Royal Monastery&quot;. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jayavarman-vii">Jayavarman VII</a> founded it as a Mahayana Buddhist monastery and centre of learning, one of the first temples of his great building programme.</p><p>Its main image, of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/prajnaparamita">Prajnaparamita</a>, the personification of wisdom, was modelled on the king's mother. The northern and southern satellite temples were dedicated to the king's guru, Jayamangalartha, and to his elder brother. The foundation stele records that more than 12,500 people lived at the site, among them 18 high priests and 615 dancers, with a further 80,000 in surrounding villages providing services and supplies, and that the temple amassed gold, pearls and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silk">silks</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">What it is</h2><p>Ta Prohm is a flat <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khmer-empire">Khmer</a> temple, not a temple-mountain. Five rectangular walls enclose a central sanctuary, and the temple faces east, so that the sanctuary lies to the west along a long east-west axis. The three inner enclosures are galleried. Face towers like those of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bayon">Bayon</a> were added to the gateways in the thirteenth century, and building went on as late as the reign of Srindravarman at the end of that century.</p><p>There are few narrative reliefs compared with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/angkor-wat">Angkor Wat</a> or <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/angkor-thom">Angkor Thom</a>, but a badly eroded one shows the Great Departure of Siddhartha, the future Buddha, and others show devatas, meditating ascetics and temple guardians. The temple was built without mortar. After it was abandoned, trees took root in the loosened stones.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Discovery and study</h2><p>The temple was abandoned after the fall of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khmer-empire">Khmer Empire</a> in the fifteenth century. When conservation of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/angkor">Angkor</a> temples began in the early twentieth century, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ecole-francaise-dextreme-orient">École française d'Extrême-Orient</a> chose to leave Ta Prohm largely as it was found, as a concession to the general taste for the picturesque. Much work has since gone into stabilising the ruins and keeping their look of neglect.</p><p>Two kinds of tree dominate the ruins, though sources disagree on their identification: the larger is either the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silk">silk</a>-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cotton">cotton</a> tree or Tetrameles nudiflora, the smaller either a strangler fig or the gold apple. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/unesco">UNESCO</a> inscribed Ta Prohm on the World Heritage List in 1992. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/archaeological-survey-of-india">Archaeological Survey of India</a> and the APSARA Authority carry out its conservation.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Ta Prohm” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>An inscription at the temple of <b>Ta Prohm</b> records thousands of villages and attendants assigned to its upkeep, showing how temples worked as economic centres.</blockquote><a href="/empire/khmer-empire">Khmer Empire · At a glance</a></li><li><blockquote>For most of the Angkor period Buddhism came after Hinduism in popularity, though kings built Buddhist temples and monasteries beside their Hindu shrines, and Jayavarman VII founded <b>Ta Prohm</b> for his mother and Preah Khan for his father.</blockquote><a href="/societies/khmer-empire">Social Hierarchy of the Khmer Empire</a></li></ul></section>
